{
  "slug": "copula",
  "title": "Copula",
  "definition": "A verb or particle that links the [term:subject] to a non-verbal [term:predicate]. In English the copula is a form of 'be'. In many languages the copula is absent in the present tense, producing a [term:verbless-clause]. Crosslinguistically, copulas take several forms: (a) a full verb (Estonian on, Russian быть); (b) a verb present only in certain tense/aspect combinations; (c) an auxiliary; (d) an invariant particle (Mandarin shì, Turkish im-). Copular verbs tend to be highly irregular, 'semi-verbal' (lacking some verbal morphology), and often develop from demonstrative pronouns or perception verbs. They also frequently double as auxiliaries.",
  "relatedTerms": ["verbless-clause", "predicate", "subject", "classification-predicate", "equative-predicate"],
